  "query_preview": "..\nA couple of persistent, slow moving T-storms pushed both severe\nand flash flooding warning limits late this evening in the \nLower Susquehanna Valley. Upper shortwave progressing through \nthe Mid Atlantic could linger isolated convection early into \nthe overnight hours. Areas of fog should expand in coverage \nthrough predawn into daybreak early Monday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms capable of damaging winds \nand heavy rainfall are possible Monday and Tuesday \n\nThere is still some uncertainty regarding the coverage of \nstorms during the day on Monday as upper level forcing is \nexpected to remain weak. We should see at least scattered \nthunderstorm development during the afternoon as modest \ninstability develops, but shear will remain weak. The severe \nthreat appears to be low, but a few strong to severe wind gusts \nwill be possible with any stronger storms. Some models show an \nMCS diving south from the Great Lakes Monday night which could \nimpact western portions of the area, but confidence is low on \ntiming and placement of this feature.\n\nTuesday will likely see the most widespread showers and\nthunderstorms of the week as a potent shortwave approaches from\nthe west and its associated surface low tracks over the region.\nShowers and storms will likely be ongoing across parts of\nCentral PA Tuesday morning and should continue through the rest\nof the day. The potential for severe weather remains uncertain\nas it will depend on much we are able to destabilize behind the\nmorning showers and storms. SPC has areas roughly along and \nsouth of I-80 in a marginal risk for severe weather and they\nhave included far southern portions of the area in a slight\nrisk.\n\nHeavy rain will also be a concern Monday evening into Tuesday\nwith any storms that develop as PWAT values will climb to around\n1.8 inches. Storms will be very efficient rainfall producers and\nareas that see repeated rounds of thunderstorms may see some\nflash flooding concerns develop. WPC has placed nearly all of\nCentral PA in a marginal risk for excessive rainfall for both\nMonday and Tuesday.",
